artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider the following observational situation in extragalactic astrophysics. A growing sample of star-forming galaxies at high redshift (z ≳ 6, in the reionization era) shows nebular emission-line spectra implying nitrogen-to-oxygen abundance ratios log(N/O) that are markedly elevated compared to the bulk of local star-forming galaxies and H II regions at comparable metallicity. In the (log(N/O), log(O/H)) plane, these galaxies cluster between the equilibrium abundance pattern of the CN cycle and that of the full CNO cycle of hydrogen burning, a locus that can in principle be reached at normal C/O if material processed through partial CNO burning is selectively expelled.

Identify and discriminate among the following candidate physical mechanisms that could account for this strong N/O enhancement:

(a) unusually efficient and bursty star formation histories, which can transiently raise N/O via differential timescales of N and O production;
(b) the galaxies being observed progenitors of present-day globular clusters, in which the conditions for the multiple-population N enhancement seen in old globular clusters are operating;
(c) enrichment driven by extremely massive stars (M ≳ a few hundred M_⊙) or supermassive stars (M ≳ 10^3–10^5 M_⊙) whose hot-bottom or interior CNO-processed material is shed via winds;
(d) enrichment by ordinary massive stars (≈ 8–100 M_⊙) and very massive stars (≈ 100–300 M_⊙) through stellar winds and binary mass transfer, with no need for an exotic population;
(e) systematic biases in the nebular abundance diagnostics, stellar-evolution tracks, or stellar-feedback prescriptions used to interpret the spectra.

Taking into account that high-redshift gas-phase metallicities are determined almost exclusively from oxygen lines, while stellar population-synthesis models adopted to interpret the ionizing continuum depend sensitively on the iron abundance (which is decoupled from oxygen by the differing nucleosynthetic timescales of core-collapse and Type Ia supernovae), determine which of (a)–(e), or which combination thereof, is required to consistently reproduce the observed N/O versus O/H locus and the associated UV/optical line ratios of the high-redshift N-rich galaxies.

